# Observability

## Overview

Every Agent Server Turn emits `:telemetry` events with a fixed set of names,
measurements, and metadata. Jido also keeps a short Turn history per Agent,
writes optional semantic log lines, and maps Turns to OpenTelemetry spans when
your application runs an SDK. This Livebook attaches a handler, runs live
Turns, and shows what each source reports.

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido, "~> 3.0.0-beta.2"}])
```

## An Agent to Watch

The counter returns one `Emit` Directive per Turn, so the Turn has effects to
observe. `default_dispatch:` sends the emitted Signal to this Livebook
process.

```elixir
defmodule Watch.Counter do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "watched_counter"

  state do
    schema(Zoi.object(%{count: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.default(0)}))
  end

  routes do
    signal_source("/watch")

    route "counter.increment", as: :increment, defaults: %{amount: 1} do
      action %{amount: amount},
        schema: Zoi.object(%{amount: Zoi.integer()}),
        context: context do
        count = context.agent_state.count + amount

        {:ok, %{context.agent_state | count: count},
         [Jido.Agent.Directive.emit("counter.changed", %{count: count})]}
      end
    end
  end
end
```

## Attach a Handler

`Jido.Telemetry.metrics/0` lists the event catalog as `Telemetry.Metrics`
definitions. The handler below forwards each event to this process.

```elixir
defmodule Watch.Handler do
  def handle(event, measurements, metadata, test_pid) do
    send(test_pid, {:jido_event, event, measurements, metadata})
  end
end

events =
  Jido.Telemetry.metrics()
  |> Enum.map(& &1.event_name)
  |> Enum.uniq()
  |> Kernel.++([[:jido, :agent_server, :turn, :start]])

:telemetry.attach_many("watch-guide", events, &Watch.Handler.handle/4, self())
```

The result is `:ok`. Use a module function as the handler. `:telemetry`
logs a performance warning for anonymous functions.

## Run a Turn

`debug: true` turns on the Turn history for this Agent.

```elixir
{:ok, _jido} = Jido.start()

{:ok, counter} =
  Jido.start_agent(Watch.Counter,
    id: "watched-1",
    debug: true,
    default_dispatch: {:pid, target: self()}
  )

signal = Watch.Counter.increment_signal!(%{amount: 2})
{:ok, agent, _directives} = Jido.AgentServer.call(counter, signal)
agent.state.count
```

The result is `2`. Collect the events:

```elixir
collect = fn collect, acc ->
  receive do
    {:jido_event, event, measurements, metadata} ->
      collect.(collect, [{event, measurements, metadata} | acc])
  after
    100 -> Enum.reverse(acc)
  end
end

seen = collect.(collect, [])
Enum.map(seen, fn {event, _, _} -> Enum.join(event, ".") end)
```

The list holds the events in order:

```text
jido.agent_server.lifecycle.start
jido.agent_server.turn.start
jido.agent_server.commit
jido.agent_server.directives.returned
jido.agent_server.turn.stop
jido.agent_server.turn.settled
jido.agent_server.effects.start
jido.agent_server.effects.stop
```

## The Event Catalog

| Event | When | Measurements |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `[:jido, :agent_server, :lifecycle, :start]` | The Server starts | time |
| `[:jido, :agent_server, :lifecycle, :stop]` | The Server stops | time |
| `[:jido, :agent_server, :turn, :start]` | A Turn is admitted | time |
| `[:jido, :agent_server, :commit]` | A candidate is committed | revisions, `directive_count` |
| `[:jido, :agent_server, :directives, :returned]` | Directives are queued | `directive_count` |
| `[:jido, :agent_server, :turn, :stop]` | Evaluation and commit end | `duration`, revisions, `directive_count` |
| `[:jido, :agent_server, :turn, :exception]` | The Turn raises | `duration` |
| `[:jido, :agent_server, :turn, :settled]` | The Turn result is final | revisions, `directive_count` |
| `[:jido, :agent_server, :effects, :start]` | Directives start | `directive_count` |
| `[:jido, :agent_server, :effects, :stop]` | Directives end | `directive_count`, `directive_completed` |
| `[:jido, :persistence, :operation, :start \| :stop \| :exception]` | A storage call | `duration` |

Revisions are `state_version_before` and `state_version_after`. `duration`
is in native time units. Point events carry `system_time` and
`monotonic_time`.

Look at the Turn stop event:

```elixir
{_event, measurements, metadata} =
  Enum.find(seen, fn {event, _, _} -> event == [:jido, :agent_server, :turn, :stop] end)

{Map.keys(measurements) |> Enum.sort(),
 Map.take(metadata, [:agent_id, :status, :stage, :committed?, :schema_version])}
```

The result is:

```text
{[:directive_count, :duration, :state_version_after, :state_version_before],
 %{status: :ok, agent_id: "watched-1", schema_version: 1, stage: :commit,
   committed?: true}}
```

Metadata also holds `turn_id`, `signal_id`, `trace_id`, and `span_id`.

## Metadata Rules

Metadata is an allowlist of scalar values. It never holds Agent or Plugin
state, Signal or Directive data, raw errors, process handles, credentials,
or caller context. Fixed fields use fixed values:

| Field | Values |
| --- | --- |
| `status` | `:ok`, `:error`, `:cancelled`, `:timed_out`, `:conflict`, `:indeterminate`, `:not_found`, `:rejected` |
| `stage` | `:evaluate`, `:commit`, `:after_commit`, `:directive` |
| `committed?` | `true` or `false` |
| `schema_version` | `1` |

Errors add `error_type` and, for stable codes, `error_code`.

## A Failed Turn

```elixir
bad = Watch.Counter.increment_signal!(%{amount: "two"})
{:error, _reason} = Jido.AgentServer.call(counter, bad)

for {event, _, metadata} <- collect.(collect, []) do
  {Enum.join(event, "."), Map.take(metadata, [:status, :stage, :committed?])}
end
```

The Turn fails in evaluation, before commit:

```text
[{"jido.agent_server.turn.start", %{}},
 {"jido.agent_server.turn.stop", %{status: :error, stage: :evaluate, committed?: false}},
 {"jido.agent_server.turn.settled", %{status: :error, stage: :evaluate, committed?: false}}]
```

No commit, Directive, or effects events follow.

## Metrics

Pass `Jido.Telemetry.metrics/0` to any `Telemetry.Metrics` reporter in your
supervision tree:

```elixir
Jido.Telemetry.metrics()
|> Enum.map(fn metric -> {Enum.join(metric.name, "."), metric.tags} end)
```

The result has 13 definitions:

| Metric | Type | Tags |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `jido.agent_server.turn.stop.count` | counter | `status`, `stage` |
| `jido.agent_server.turn.stop.duration` | summary, ms | `status`, `stage` |
| `jido.persistence.operation.stop.count` | counter | `operation`, `status`, `persistence_reason` |
| `jido.persistence.operation.stop.duration` | summary, ms | same |
| `jido.persistence.operation.exception.count` | counter | same |
| `jido.persistence.operation.exception.duration` | summary, ms | same |
| `jido.agent_server.lifecycle.start.count` | counter | none |
| `jido.agent_server.lifecycle.stop.count` | counter | none |
| `jido.agent_server.commit.count` | counter | `status` |
| `jido.agent_server.directives.returned.count` | counter | none |
| `jido.agent_server.effects.start.count` | counter | none |
| `jido.agent_server.effects.stop.count` | counter | `status`, `stage` |
| `jido.agent_server.turn.settled.count` | counter | `status`, `stage` |

Tags never contain Agent, Signal, Turn, or trace IDs, so the metric
cardinality stays fixed.

## Turn History

`status/1` returns the last Turn outcome. With `debug: true`, `history/1`
returns the last 100 outcomes. Set `debug_max_events:` (1 to 10,000) to keep
a different number.

```elixir
Jido.AgentServer.history(counter)
|> Enum.map(&Map.take(&1, [:status, :stage, :committed?, :state_version_after]))
```

The result has one entry per Turn, oldest first:

```text
[%{status: :succeeded, stage: :commit, committed?: true, state_version_after: 1},
 %{status: :failed, stage: :running, committed?: false, state_version_after: 1}]
```

History uses `:succeeded` and `:failed` for `status`, and the Server phase
for `stage`. The failed Turn shows `stage: :running`, where telemetry reports
`stage: :evaluate`. Each outcome also holds `turn_id`, `signal_id`, `directive_count`, and
`duration_ms`. It never holds state or Signal data. Without `debug: true`,
`history/1` returns `[]`.

## Semantic Logs

Jido attaches a log handler, `"jido-semantic-logger"`, at startup. It is off
by default. Turn it on in config:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
# config/runtime.exs
config :jido, :telemetry,
  semantic_log_mode: :interesting,
  semantic_slow_threshold_ms: 1_000
```

| Mode | Logs |
| --- | --- |
| `:off` | Nothing. The default. |
| `:errors` | Failed, cancelled, and timed-out events, at `:warning`. |
| `:interesting` | Errors, and events slower than `semantic_slow_threshold_ms`. |
| `:all` | Every catalog event. Successful events log at `:debug`. |

The handler reads the setting on each event:

```elixir
Application.put_env(:jido, :telemetry, semantic_log_mode: :errors)
Jido.AgentServer.call(counter, bad)
```

The Livebook output shows two warnings:

```text
[warning] [jido.semantic] event=agent_server.turn.stop agent_id=watched-1
  committed?=false ... stage=evaluate status=error ...
[warning] [jido.semantic] event=agent_server.turn.settled ...
```

Each line uses the same allowlisted fields as the telemetry metadata.

### Instance Debug Level

`Jido.debug/1` and `MyApp.Jido.debug/1` set a per-instance log level. `:on`
uses the `:interesting` mode and `:verbose` uses `:all`. The `debug:`
instance option sets it at startup.

The level applies only to events that name their instance. In this release,
persistence events do and Agent Server events don't, so `Jido.debug(:verbose)`
doesn't add Turn log lines. Use `semantic_log_mode` for Turn logs.

```elixir
Jido.debug(:verbose)
Jido.debug()
```

The result is `:verbose`.

## Trace Context

Each Turn runs in a W3C trace. When the Signal carries a `traceparent`, the
Turn joins that trace. Otherwise the Turn starts a new one. Emitted Signals
carry a child trace, so the next Agent's Turn joins the same trace.

```elixir
trace = Jido.Signal.Trace.new()
traced = Watch.Counter.increment_signal!(%{amount: 1})
{:ok, traced} = Jido.Signal.Trace.put(traced, trace)

{:ok, _agent, _} = Jido.AgentServer.call(counter, traced)

{_event, _, metadata} =
  Enum.find(collect.(collect, []), fn {event, _, metadata} ->
    event == [:jido, :agent_server, :turn, :stop] and metadata.signal_id == traced.id
  end)

{metadata.trace_id == trace.trace_id, metadata.parent_span_id == trace.span_id}
```

The result is `{true, true}`: the Turn's span is a child of the Signal's
span, in the same trace.

## OpenTelemetry

Jido maps Turns and persistence operations to OpenTelemetry spans when:

- your application depends on `opentelemetry_api` and runs an SDK such as
  `opentelemetry`; and
- `config :jido, :opentelemetry, enabled: false` isn't set.

Jido doesn't start the SDK or configure sampling and export.
`Jido.Telemetry.open_telemetry?/0` reports whether the mapping is active:

```elixir
Jido.Telemetry.open_telemetry?()
```

The result here is `false`, because this Livebook has no SDK.

## Clean Up

```elixir
Application.delete_env(:jido, :telemetry)
Jido.debug(:off)
:telemetry.detach("watch-guide")
:ok = Jido.stop_agent(counter)
```

```elixir
Jido.stop()
```

The last result is `:ok`.

